ssion  so that's one example as  now in our many stories the first 20  lessons are all in the present tense  but thereafter we introduce other tenses  we introduce the subjunctive and so you  may very well want to save examples of  the subjunctive examples of  past tense or anything that you're  having trouble with one thing i should  point out is that  in any of these  let's say we go to prepara  you can look up other dictionaries all  right if you look up other dictionaries  not only can you look for other  dictionaries but you can also look up  the conjugation  now i have to drag this in to my screen  here prepara okay here you have prepara  tells you where you are it's the third  person  and so you can see the conjugation if  you were in the imperfect or the future  or the conditional you would be able to  see that right away so one of the  advantages of working online is that you  can immediately  uh  you know find out more information like  like if we go to toma  all right takes we look up dictionary  we have two different uh we there's a  whole variety of dictionaries that you  can choose from but i find that this  conjugator is very useful so toma thomas  and you can look up the uh conjugation  so  i believe that with this kind of help  and uh even though the stories is a  little more difficult and but if you go  through the story  uh part a which is miguel escobinero  then we get this section b where  milevanto here miguel gets up and we  start to get used to the fact that  because it's levanto levantas levanta it  changes we don't need to say miguel here  melevanto i get out i get up he doesn't  say  levanto he says he doesn't i get up he  just says  and so we have the same vocabulary again  a second time but this time the story is  told by miguel and then finally we start  to get questions with again the same  vocabulary miguel cerebanta and we've  already gotten used to that phrase allah  says todas las mananas there's a  statement a question celebanta m